<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="dhurrin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-861" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Cyanogenic glycosides are phytotoxic secondary metabolites found in several thousand plant species including a number of agriculturally important crops such as cassava, sorghum, flax and lotus. Chemically they are &amp;beta;-glycosides of &amp;alpha;-hydroxynitriles. They are derived from amino acids, including tyrosine, phenylalanine, valine, leucine, isoleucine, and cyclopentenyl-glycine. Generally, a particular cyanogenic glycoside occurs only in one or two plant families, and conversely, only one or two cyanogenic glycosides are present in a given plant. In order to avoid self-intoxication cyanogenic glucosides are stored in the vacuole. Under pathogen or herbivore attacks they come in contact with activating enzymes (specific &amp;beta;-glucosidases and &amp;alpha;-hydroxynitrile lyases) that reside in the cytoplasm or apoplastic space of the cells, releasing toxic |FRAME: HCN| that serves as the first line of chemical defense |CITS:[16667728][Ganjewala10]|. While the most studied pathway of cyanogenic glucoside biosynthesis is that for |FRAME: CPD-1042|, all cyanogenic glycosides are synthesized from amino acids by conserved mechanisms, and the pathways only differ in the specificity of the enzymes involved. The pathways involve two cytochrome P450s and a glucosyltransferase. The first step, the conversion of the amino acid to an oxime, is catalyzed by a membrane-bound multifunctional cytochrome P450 that belongs to the CYP79 family. This is the rate-limiting step of the pathway, and the substrate preference of this enzyme is the major factor determining the content of different cyanogenic glucosides accumulated in a given species. The next step, the conversion of the aldoxime into a hydroxynitrile by dehydration and C-hydroxylation is catalyzed by a different membrane-bound multifunctional cytochrome P450 that belong to the CYP71 family. The substrate for the dehydration reaction is the (&lt;i&gt;Z&lt;/i&gt;) isomer of the oxime, while the isomer produced by the CYP79 enzyme is known tio be the (&lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;) isomer. While the two isomers do reach equilibrium without catalysis, the reaction is very slow and early observations indicated that the isomerization is catalyzed &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; by an enzyme |CITS: [2684955]|. The identity of that enzyme responsible for the isomerization was revealed only in 2015, when it was shown to be one of the enzymatic activities of CYP71 enzymes |CITS: [26361733]|. Thus CYP71 enzymes are trifunctional, catalyzing isomerization, dehydration and C-hydroxylation. The last part of the pathway is catalyzed by soluble UDP-sugar glycosyltransferases that glycosylate the hydroxynitriles into cyanogenic glycosides. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CPD-1042 Dhurrin| is a cyanogenic glucoside produced by seedlings of |FRAME:TAX-4558| |CITS: [11312134] [10585420]|. The biosynthetic pathway of |FRAME: CPD-1042| was shown to involve |FRAME: TYR|, |FRAME: CPD-267|, and (4-hydroxyphenyl)acetaldehyde oxime as early intermediates |CITS: [468842]|. A study with a microsomal preparation obtained from etiolated seedlings of sorghum has shown that the pathway produces initially the (E)-isomer of (4-hydroxyphenyl)acetaldehyde oxime, which is converted to the (Z) isomer |CITS: [2684955]|. The conversion of |FRAME: TYR| to |FRAME: CPD-2205| is catalyzed by a cytochrome P-450 enzyme, |FRAME: G-452| |CITS: [16668136][7937883]|. The activity involves two successive &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-hydroxylation reactions, producing |FRAME: CPD-7070| as an intermediate |CITS: [7876084]|. A second cytochrome P-450, |FRAME: G-453|, then converts the (E) isomer to the (Z) isomer, and converts it in two steps to |FRAME: CPD-648| |CITS: [9414567][26361733]|, which is glucosylated to form |FRAME: CPD-1042| |CITS: [10585420]|.</Description>
